[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the HTTP service (Apache2) on the internal server srv1, which also hosts internal DNS.

Mechanism: Name resolution was functioning, but the web service itself was down. On client PCs, DNS resolved srv1.jeremysitlab.com to 10.5.0.4 (evidence: pc1/pc2/pc3 getent returned “10.5.0.4 srv1.jeremysitlab.com” and ping to the name succeeded). However, initial web fetches failed because there was no HTTP listener on srv1 (evidence: on srv1, apache2.service was “inactive (dead)” and ss showed “NO_HTTP_LISTEN” for :80/:443). This presents to non-technical users as “site can’t be found,” even though DNS was up; the browser simply couldn’t reach a web server at the resolved address.

Localization: srv1 (10.5.0.4), Apache2 daemon, TCP port 80.

Root cause: The Apache2 service on srv1 was not running.

Corrective fix: I started Apache2 on srv1. Post-fix verification shows apache2 is “active” and enabled, and ss now shows “LISTEN 0.0.0.0:80”. Clients pc1/pc2/pc3 can retrieve the Apache default page from http://srv1.jeremysitlab.com. DNS remains healthy on srv1 (port 53 was listening before and after). Recommendation: check srv1’s logs (journalctl -u apache2) to determine why Apache stopped, and keep monitoring to ensure it remains running. Consider watchdog/monitoring to auto-restart the service if it fails. No network/routing issues were observed.
=== END ===